Example 6
The following example adds an internal clock with a non-integer multiplication factor:
SETUP> add_clocks clk -period 40 ns
SETUP> add_clocks /path/to/pll/o -reference clk -freq_mult 610 -freq_div 25 -label clk610
The primary input clock has a frequency of 25 MHz. The internal clock has a frequency of 610 
MHz. Therefore, the multiplication factor is 610/25, or 24.4.
The value for the -freq_multiplier argument must be an integer, so you use -freq_multiplier and 
-freq_divider together to specify the internal clock’s frequency.
The following provides an equivalent result:
SETUP> add_clocks /path/to/pll/o -reference clk -freq_mult 122 -freq_div 5 -label clk610
Example 7
The following example works in “patterns -ijtag” only. The following commands produce an 
internal half-speed clock on the ICL module “chip” during ICL extraction:
set_design_sources -format icl -y icl_data
set_current_design chip
add_clocks { pll_block/clock1 } -reference clock1 -freq_divider 2
set_system_mode analysis
The resulting ICL created by the ICL extraction looks as follows:
Module chip {
   // Created by ICL extraction
   ClockPort clock1;
   …
   Instance block1_I1 Of block1 {
      InputPort ClkA = add_clock_inst_0.ClockOut;
       …
   }
   Instance add_clock_inst_0 Of chip_add_clock_0 {
      InputPort ClockIn = clock1;
   }
}
