the parameter in each instance of the design module. If the default value of the parameter in the 
ICL module does not match the value from the design instance, the tool adds the same 
parameter override in the extracted ICL instances.
For example, consider a Verilog and ICL module with a parameter called bus_size that has a 
default value of 8. In the design, one instance of the module has the parameter overwritten to 6 
in its instantiation call, while another instance uses the default value. The tool creates the 
resulting ICL instance associated with the first Verilog instance with the bus_size parameter 
equal to 6 in its “Instance Of” wrapper.
Instance inst1 Of modA {
  parameter bus_size = 6;
}
Instance inst2 Of modA;
The ICL parameter equation does not support exponents. To address this limitation, you can 
define a second parameter in the Verilog module as follows, with a matching parameter in the 
ICL module:
Verilog view:
Module abc (...);
  parameter addr_count = 4;
  parameter max_addr = (2**addr_count)-1;
endmodule
ICL view:
Module abc {
    parameter addr_count = 4;
    parameter max_addr = 15;
}
If you overwrite the addr_count parameter to 8 in the Verilog instantiation, the tool 
automatically creates the following associated ICL instance:
Instance YYY Of abc {
   parameter addr_count = 8;
   parameter max_addr = 255;
}
