Wrapping hides the original RTL module definition from downstream tools that do not 
support module overrides. The following is an example of a unique, modified RTL file with 
an unmodified copy of the original module definition at the top of the file:
module reg_blk (output logic q, input logic a, b, s0, clk, rst);
  timeunit 1ns/1ns;
  logic n1; //internal signals
  mux2 i1 (.i1(a), .i0(b), .s0(s0), .q(n1));
  dffr i2 (.D(n1), .R(rst), .CLK(clk), .Q(q));
endmodule: reg_blk
module reg_blk_1
(output logic q, input logic a, b, s0, clk, rst);
  timeunit 1ns/1ns;
  logic  n1; //internal signals
  wire insertion_inverter_Y;
  mux2 i1 (.i1(a), .i0(b), .s0(s0), .q(n1));
  dffr  i2 (.D(insertion_inverter_Y), .R(rst), .CLK(clk), .Q(q));
  inv01 insertion_inverter(
      .A(n1), .Y(insertion_inverter_Y)
  );
endmodule : reg_blk_1
If you turn on wrapping, the tool wraps the copied original module definitions with “`ifdef 
TESSENT_DUPLICATE_MODULES” and “`endif” compiler directives, which Tessent 
Shell can read. These wrapped modules are hidden in your downstream flow because 
TESSENT_DUPLICATE_MODULES is not defined there. You can remove these compiler 
directives or define the TESSENT_DUPLICATE_MODULES macro if your downstream 
flow requires these modules.

