Examples
Example 1
This example shows you how to configure the DftSpecification to insert a BISR controller, read 
in a BISR segment order file, and configure the SecondaryHostChainInterface wrapper to 
specify custom port names for the pass-through connections in the current design block:
DftSpecification() {
 MemoryBisr {
    bisr_segment_order_file : tower_base.bisr_segment_order;
    Controller {
      …
    }
    SecondaryHostChainInterface {
      to_scan_in : %s_to_bisr_si;
      from_scan_out : %s_from_bisr_so;
      to_capture_shift_clock : %s_to_bisr_clk;
      to_reset : %s_to_bisr_reset;
      to_shift_en : %s_to_bisr_shift_en;
      to_memory_disable : %s_to_bisr_mem_disable;
      to_memory_chain_select : %s_to_bisr_mem_chain_select;
    }
 }
}
The BISR segment order file for this example is as follows:
BisrSegmentOrderSpecification {
  PowerDomainGroup(-) {
    OrderedElements {
      mem1;
      mem2;
    }
  }
  PowerDomainGroup(G1) {
    OrderedElements {
      mem3;
      mem4;
      SecondaryHostChainInterface(G1);
    }
  }
  PowerDomainGroup(G2) {
    OrderedElements {
      SecondaryHostChainInterface(G2);
    }
  }
}
The SecondaryHostChainInterface(interface_name) label can be any descriptive string. 
Common names are “pdgA_top”, “pdgB_right”, and so on. When you only have one power 
domain group, you can remove the power domain ID as part of the interface name (for example: 
“top”, “right”). In this example, it simply replicates the associated power domain group label.
