Examples
The following example extracts the repair status and repair solution from all repairable 
memories.
column Specifies that all single- bit errors are repaired by redundant column or IO elements; 
multi-bit errors are automatically repaired by spare row elements. If the number of 
faults exceeds the number of redundant columns or IO elements, the remaining 
redundant row elements are allocated for these faults. This is the most flexible 
solution because it automatically allocates redundant row elements for multi-bit 
failures while allocating redundant column or IO elements for single-bit failures. 
A redundant column or IO element cannot repair a multi-bit failure. If only a 
redundant column or IO element is available when a multi-bit error is detected, the 
repair status is unrepairable.
row Specifies that redundant rows are allocated for all errors (single-bit and multi-bit). 
The redundant column/IO elements are only allocated for the remaining faults after 
all redundant rows are allocated. 

